It seems I've made a historical error.

My friend (and extropian-list lurker) Eric Tiedemann informs me that
Korzybski's General Semantics cannot be derivative of Wittgenstein's
natural-language analysis for the simple reason that GS predates the
latter's _Philosophical_Investigations_ by twenty years.  Ooops...

This has the interesting consequence that Korzybski looks more like a
truly original thinker and less like just an adept systematizer of other
peoples' ideas.  GS is indeed very similar in spirit and method to
the late Wittgenstein, but Korzybski must have arrived there independently.

The connection to Moore is probably still valid, however.  And there are
good reasons to believe that Charles Pierce and the "Vienna Circle" were
very influential in AK's thinking.
