Subject: Email bombs (was: alt.extropians created)

> From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
> Ah!  The email bomb strategy!  I recall suggesting that the Politeness
> Society use a similar method in order to give its sanctions bite against
> lurkers.  I would be interested to know if anyone is familiar with email
> bombs having actually been put to use.  Were adequate defenses, such as
> mail filters, put in place?  

Typical mail-bomb incidents happen in response to a particularly
atrocious Usenet post, which prompts a large number of people to
reply at length -- with, in some cases, /usr/dict/words.  This
causes various problems on the target system, ranging from shortage
of disk space to a crash of the mail transport system resulting in
the whole machine falling over.  Solo mailbombing would hardly be
effective; you're loading your machine as much as the other guy's.
And bombing a Usenet group is pretty pointless; it would be hard to
tell from normal traffic.

> Note that email bombers face retaliation problems: unless you send
> the bomb anonymously or set up screens, you face email bombs in
> return.

Send no bombs through the jarthur remailer, thanks.

> It's an interesting topic, I think, one that can tell us something about
> spontaneous ordering and game theory.

The spontaneous order which has evolved is one which treats
deliberate mailbombing as an anti-social act -- rightly, IMHO.  It
wastes bandwidth, may affect everybody on the system, and can slag
other machines than the target.  Socially acceptable attacks should
be more finely aimed.

WRT alt.extropians: don't worry about it.  Bogus alt groups don't
get much propagation these days.

